data/silver/background/
**/__pycache__/
data/gold/results/**/*.tmp
data/gold/results/**/~$*
scripts/notebooks/.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.DS_Store
*.pyc
.pylint.d
data/bronze/exiobase/IOT_2016_ixi
# Per-release EXIOBASE symlink trees (data/bronze/exiobase/<release>/IOT_<year>_ixi).
# Same reasoning as the top-level symlink above: a quarter of a gigabyte of
# third-party data per release, held once outside the working copy and pointed
# at from here. A directory pattern, because the release folder is a real
# directory holding symlinks rather than a symlink itself.
data/bronze/exiobase/v3_7/
data/bronze/exiobase/v3_8_2/
# Total-output vectors fetched per release for the release-choice comparison,
# one x.txt per release and table year. Untracked for two reasons: they are
# EXIOBASE redistribution, and from v3.9 onwards EXIOBASE is under a customised
# non-commercial licence that the CC-BY-SA terms of v3.7/v3.8.2 do not cover.
# The DERIVED comparison - Danish health output against the national accounts,
# 21 numbers - is published in gold, which is reporting a research finding
# rather than redistributing the database. Re-fetch with
# `analysis.fetch_release_output_vectors`, which pulls only x.txt out of each
# remote archive by HTTP range request: 0.46 MB instead of 0.76 GB.
data/bronze/exiobase/release_output_vectors/

# Large external MRIO downloads: reproducible from the documented URLs
data/bronze/eurostat_figaro/estat_naio_10_fcp_ii4_*.tsv.gz
# No trailing slash: a working copy may hold this as a symlink to another
# checkout's model pickles rather than a plain directory, and a slash-suffixed
# pattern only matches a directory, not a symlink of that name.
data/silver/background
.venv/

# Handoff workbooks between main_2025.py and the three modules that read them
# back. They are regenerable intermediates, not a deliverable, so they belong in
# silver, not gold - and are ignored explicitly here rather than relying on a
# personal global excludesFile, which is exactly the mechanism that once hid a
# required input file from this repository.
data/silver/handoff/*
# ...but the folder's own readme is tracked, because it is the only thing
# that says what these workbooks are and why they are not gold. A negation
# cannot reach inside an excluded DIRECTORY, so the pattern above excludes
# the folder's contents rather than the folder itself.
!data/silver/handoff/readme.md

# The named-column form of the Danish medicines register: 78 MB per year,
# rebuilt from the tracked bronze original by analysis.build_atc_sales. Ignored
# explicitly for the same reason as the line above: the global excludesFile
# already drops every .csv, but relying on a machine-local file to hide 156 MB
# is relying on a setting this repository cannot see.
data/silver/dk_medicines_register/dk_atc_sales_*.csv

# The user's global gitignore excludes *.csv. The gold results ARE the
# deliverable — detail tables alongside aggregates — so un-ignore them here.
# Repository .gitignore takes precedence over core.excludesFile.
!data/gold/results/**/*.csv
!data/gold/results/*.csv
!data/gold/results/**/*.csv.gz
!data/gold/results/**/*.parquet
!data/gold/results/*.parquet

# Excel lock files
~$*

# The global excludesFile also drops *.tiff. Publication figures ARE the
# deliverable, so un-ignore the figures tree (repo .gitignore takes precedence).
!figures/**/*.tiff
!figures/**/*.png

# The bibliography's single source of truth. analysis.bibliography reads it
# and audit check C8 verifies every in-text citation against it, so a clone
# without it cannot run the check that guards the reference list.
!docs/references.csv

# Bronze register inputs. These are small, public, and the pipeline cannot be
# reproduced without them, so they belong in version control even though the
# global excludesFile drops every .csv.
#
# The pattern reaches EVERY subfolder, not just the bronze root. The previous
# rule un-ignored `data/bronze/*.csv` only, so the moment a register landed in
# a subfolder the global excludesFile swallowed it: that is how
# `dst_capital_stock/nabk69_health_assets_2022.csv` sat on one machine's disk,
# read by `analysis.capital_gfcf`, and absent from every clone.
!data/bronze/**/*.csv
!data/bronze/*.csv

# Silver products that ARE in version control. Silver is regenerable, so most
# of it is ignored: the model-object store, the register, the handoff workbooks.
# These few are tracked because they are small, because they are the numbers the
# manuscript's expenditure and bottom-up terms rest on, and because a clone that
# has them can read what the pipeline was last run on without rebuilding it.
# Named one pattern at a time rather than as `!data/silver/**/*.csv`, so
# un-ignoring them cannot drag the 78 MB register in with them.
!data/silver/dst_supply_use/dk_data_*.csv
!data/silver/dst_supply_use/dk_expenditure_breakdown_*.csv
# phi, the sea-transport reallocation's target share. It is a PUBLISHED number:
# the methods text quotes it, layer 10's diagnostics carry it on every row, and
# it multiplies a row block of A before a Leontief inversion, so it is quoted to
# the last bit. Tracked at 401 bytes so a reader can check the published share
# against the workbook without rebuilding the layer. The other three products of
# analysis.build_shipping_inputs stay ignored; data/silver/readme.md says why,
# file by file.
!data/silver/dst_input_output/dst_water_transport_domestic_share.csv
# The FIGARO dimension table. Tracked because it is the only thing in the
# repository that says what a FIGARO code means: the fact tables are keys and a
# value, so without it a reader of layer 06 cannot tell Q86 from Q87_88. 58 kB.
# The nine Eurostat codelists it is built from are tracked too, in bronze, as
# retrieved -- they are Eurostat open data, 920 kB, and tracking them is what
# makes a rebuild offline and the label on every row checkable against the
# register it was read from.
!data/silver/eurostat_figaro/figaro_dimensions.csv
# The two FIGARO series, 1.1 MB together. Tracked because each is the whole
# benchmark a reviewer would ask to see - the Danish health input recipe across
# 2016/2019/2022/2024, and the Danish footprint across an unbroken 2016-2023 -
# and because rebuilding them needs eight dissemination-API queries and the
# 31 MB of bronze extracts those return.
!data/silver/eurostat_figaro/figaro_dk_health_inputs.csv
!data/silver/eurostat_figaro/figaro_dk_footprint_series.csv
